@*
    A whole metadata block, laid out as the record it is rather than as the code
    fence it was written in.

    Two tiers, because the block is two things. The status answers the question a
    reader asks of a chapter first — is this current? — so it shares the line
    with the heading it describes, held to the right edge of it, rather than
    waiting its turn as row one of a list. Everything else is true but secondary:
    it sits underneath, quieter and tighter, close enough to be taken in with the
    same glance.

    The status is a StatusSelector wherever it can honestly be one — the same
    control the knowledge panels already edit a chapter's status with — and the
    static badge everywhere else. Both are the application's status badge, and
    both take the modifier from the same MetadataStatusVocabulary, so the word is
    drawn the same whether or not it happens to be editable.

    A select is only honest when every state it can show is
    a state the surface actually allows and the value it is showing is one of
    them, so it appears when a vocabulary was given *and* the current status is in
    it. Neither of the other two cases can clear that bar:
    a block with no vocabulary has nothing to list, and a status the vocabulary
    does not define is not in the list — a select would either drop it silently for
    the first option or quietly stop flagging the typo the badge exists to flag.
    A block that states no status still shows nothing at all, because a select
    defaulted to the first value would be inventing a state the file never gave.

    The vocabulary arrives from the caller and is not looked up here. It used to be
    a KnowledgeFolder, which made this component — and the two shapes over it —
    unusable by anything outside the five knowledge folders, for one field's sake.
    A knowledge caller passes `KnowledgeStatus.Vocabulary(folder)` and gets exactly
    what it got before; anything else passes its own words. Nothing else about the
    record was ever folder-shaped: a field nobody writes is simply absent, which
    MetadataRecord already models.

    The secondary tier stays a description list because that is what it is — a
    field name and its value, repeated — and keeping the <dl> means a screen
    reader still pairs the two. The label goes on the wrapper and only there; it
    used to be set on this list *and* on the div MetadataBadge wraps it in, which
    announced the same block twice. The wrapper takes an explicit role because a
    bare <div> with an aria-label is a label nothing is listening for.

    A host may ask for the first tier alone. The status has a visualization a
    reader gets something from — a word with a state behind it, and a control
    where the folder allows one — and most of the second tier does not: `aliases`
    and `alternatives` are the file's own bookkeeping printed back as
    label-and-value rows, which is the raw block again with the fence taken off.
    ShowFields is how a reading surface says it wants only the tier that reads.

    Three of those rows now draw no visible label. `kind`, `version` and `effort`
    say which field they came from in the value itself — a classification chip, a
    `v` in front of a version string, a spelled-out unit on a points badge — and a
    dimmed word in front of one of those was holding up something that stands on
    its own. The label is hidden and not removed: it stays in the description list
    so the pair a screen reader depends on is still a pair. Every other row keeps
    the name, because every other row still needs it.

    Empty in, nothing out, exactly as the metadata strip behaves — and that now
    includes a block with nothing left to draw. Fields suppressed and no status
    stated leaves a bordered, labelled group holding a heading or nothing at all,
    which is a frame around an answer the block never gave.
*@

@if (RendersAnything)
{
    <div class="@ClassValue" role="group" aria-label="@AriaLabel" data-testid="@TestId" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
        <div class="knowledge-record__headline">
            @Heading
            @if (StatusIsSelectable)
            {
                <StatusSelector CurrentValue="@_status"
                                Options="@StatusOptions"
                                Slug="@Vocabulary.SlugFor(_status)"
                                OnChanged="SelectStatus" />
            }
            else
            {
                <MetadataStatusBadge Status="@_status" Vocabulary="@Vocabulary" />
            }
        </div>

        @if (ShowFields && HasSecondaryFields)
        {
            <dl class="knowledge-fields">
                @RenderReferences("related", Metadata.Related)
                @RenderReferences("depends-on", Metadata.DependsOn)
                @RenderReferences("implements", Metadata.Implements)
                @RenderAliases()
                @RenderStrings("alternatives", Metadata.Alternatives)
                @RenderKind()
                @RenderVersion()
                @RenderIssue()
                @RenderEffort()
                @RenderKeys("roadmap", Metadata.Roadmap, "knowledge-roadmap-tags")
                @RenderKeys("feature-flag", Metadata.FeatureFlag, "knowledge-feature-flag-tags")

                @* Fields the schema does not define are shown as themselves. A
                   viewer that dropped them would make a genuine schema addition
                   look like a file that says nothing.

                   Inside a code block on purpose. Razor emits the whitespace
                   between two sibling expressions as content, and trims it either
                   side of a block, so the rows above are separated by their source
                   indentation and these are not — which is the markup this record
                   has always rendered. *@
                @if (Metadata.Extra.Count > 0)
                {
                    <MetadataExtraFields Extra="@Metadata.Extra" />
                }
            </dl>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    // Each field asks its own question about emptiness and then hands its value
    // to the component for that value's shape: an absent list, a blank scalar and
    // an estimate of zero are three different answers, and only the field knows
    // which it is looking at. What a row *is* — the pair, the label, the value
    // cell — is MetadataField's, and what a value *looks like* belongs to the
    // one component per shape beside this one. What is left here is the only thing
    // that is genuinely about the record: which fields are present, and in what
    // order the rows appear.
    //
    // The value component is written hard against the row's opening tag. Razor
    // emits the whitespace between an element's tag and a child element as
    // content, so a newline there would add it to every value cell in the product.
    private RenderFragment RenderReferences(string label, IReadOnlyList<KnowledgeReference> references) =>
        @<text>
            @if (references.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="@label"><MetadataReferenceList References="@references" HrefFor="@HrefFor" OnNavigate="@OnNavigate" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // Alternatives are plain strings by design — a technology that was weighed and
    // rejected has no chapter to point at — so they never become links, and they
    // must not be dressed as one either. MetadataValueList is the component that
    // cannot: it takes no href and no handler.
    //
    // Aliases used to be drawn through here too, and are not any more: the term an
    // alias names *is* modelled somewhere, so unlike an alternative it is a word a
    // host can be asked to place. See RenderAliases.
    private RenderFragment RenderStrings(string label, IReadOnlyList<string> values) =>
        @<text>
            @if (values.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="@label"><MetadataValueList Values="@values" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // An alias applies to a name rather than standing on its own, so it is drawn as
    // a qualifier: a badge set above the baseline and a step smaller than the text
    // it belongs to.
    //
    // The record wires no resolver. HrefFor answers for a KnowledgeReference and an
    // alias is not one — an alias is a word, which is the whole reason the field
    // holds plain strings — so there is nothing here to hand down. What a host would
    // resolve an alias *to* is a question no file in this repository answers today,
    // and a parameter for it would be this component claiming to know. The
    // capability lives on MetadataAliasList, where it can be looked at and tested,
    // until a naming index exists to wire it to.
    private RenderFragment RenderAliases() =>
        @<text>
            @if (Metadata.Aliases.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="aliases"><MetadataAliasList Aliases="@Metadata.Aliases" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // A closed vocabulary drawn as the classification it is, and the first of the
    // three rows that state their own field. The label is hidden rather than
    // dropped: a chip reading `framework` says what it is to somebody looking at it,
    // and says nothing at all to somebody listening.
    private RenderFragment RenderKind() =>
        @<text>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Kind))
            {
                <MetadataField Label="kind" ShowLabel="false"><MetadataKindBadge Kind="@Metadata.Kind" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // The same authored-scalar shape it has always been, with the `v` that makes it
    // legible without a label. Still a code element: `10.0` is a version string and
    // not the number ten, and the monospace face is what stops a reader rounding it.
    private RenderFragment RenderVersion() =>
        @<text>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Version))
            {
                <MetadataField Label="version" ShowLabel="false"><MetadataScalar Value="@Metadata.Version" Prefix="v" Title="@($"version: {Metadata.Version}")" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // An issue is a URL or the `owner/repo#number` shorthand, and which of the two
    // it is decides whether it can be followed. That question belongs to the value
    // component, so the record only asks whether the field was stated at all.
    private RenderFragment RenderIssue() =>
        @<text>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Issue))
            {
                <MetadataField Label="issue"><MetadataIssueLink Issue="@Metadata.Issue" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // Absent when there is no estimate; 0 is an estimate and still shows. The
    // record asks that question here rather than leaving the badge to answer it,
    // because it is the row that must not appear — a badge that drew nothing would
    // leave a labelled empty cell.
    //
    // The third labelless row. "5 pts" names its own field; "5" would not, which is
    // why the unit was already spelled out before the label went.
    private RenderFragment RenderEffort() =>
        @<text>
            @if (Metadata.Effort is not null)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="effort" ShowLabel="false"><MetadataEffortBadge Effort="@Metadata.Effort" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // Roadmap keys and feature-flag keys are the same shape: identifiers something
    // outside these files answers to, drawn in the feature-badge family and never
    // links. One fragment for both, so the two fields cannot drift apart — and the
    // test id lands on the value cell, which is what MetadataField's TestId is
    // for.
    private RenderFragment RenderKeys(string label, IReadOnlyList<string> keys, string testId) =>
        @<text>
            @if (keys.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="@label" TestId="@testId"><MetadataKeyList Keys="@keys" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public MetadataRecord Metadata { get; set; } = MetadataRecord.Empty;

    /// <summary>The heading this record belongs to, rendered on the same line as
    /// the status. Handed in rather than faked with a negative margin: the two
    /// only genuinely share a line if they share a parent, and a host that has
    /// no heading to give simply leaves the pill on the line alone.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Heading { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The status words this surface allows, and how each is drawn.
    /// Without one the status is still shown, just not judged: the same word means
    /// different things in different places, so a tone or a warning guessed
    /// without a vocabulary would be wrong as often as right.
    ///
    /// <para>A knowledge surface passes <c>KnowledgeStatus.Vocabulary(folder)</c>.
    /// This used to be the folder itself, which is what made the whole record
    /// unavailable to a caller outside those five folders — see the note at the top
    /// of this file.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MetadataStatusVocabulary Vocabulary { get; set; } = MetadataStatusVocabulary.None;

    /// <summary>Raised with the status a reader picked, when the headline is
    /// showing a select. Optional: the view already keeps the choice on screen
    /// by itself, so a host wires this up to <em>persist</em> the change, not to
    /// make the control work.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnStatusChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a reference to a link target. The host owns the mapping
    /// from repository path to route; returning null leaves that reference as
    /// plain text or a button rather than a broken link.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? HrefFor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the secondary tier — everything that is not the status —
    /// is drawn at all. On by default, so a host that says nothing gets the whole
    /// record it has always got; off for a surface that is showing the file
    /// itself, where those fields are the fence read back as rows.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowFields { get; set; } = true;

    [Parameter]
    public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Knowledge metadata";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The status on screen, which is the authored one until a reader picks
    /// another.
    ///
    /// <para>The selector is one-way: it shows the value it is handed and reports
    /// the one that was chosen. Rendered against <c>Metadata.Status</c> directly
    /// it would snap back to the authored value on the very next render, so a
    /// reader who picked something would watch the control undo them — and with
    /// no host listening, nothing would have happened at all. Holding the choice
    /// here means the control tells the truth about itself whether or not
    /// anybody is persisting it.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? _status;

    /// <summary>
    /// The block <see cref="_status"/> was taken from, so a reader's choice
    /// survives a re-render of the same block but never carries over onto a
    /// different one — the next chapter must not open claiming a state nobody
    /// gave it.
    ///
    /// <para>Compared with the record's own equality rather than by reference,
    /// which is the weaker test of the two and so reseeds less often. Today it is
    /// not actually weaker: <c>MetadataRecord</c> is a record, but its list
    /// and dictionary members compare by reference, so two parses of the same
    /// text are already unequal. That is the safe direction — a block handed over
    /// as a fresh object is treated as a new block — and it means this keeps
    /// working, better, if that record ever gains structural equality.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private MetadataRecord? _seeded;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (Equals(_seeded, Metadata)) return;

        _seeded = Metadata;
        _status = Metadata.Status;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the headline can offer a select instead of a badge. Both halves
    /// matter: without a vocabulary there is no list of values to offer, and a
    /// status the vocabulary does not define is not on that list — putting it in a
    /// select would show the first option as if it were the file's, which is the
    /// one thing this control must never do.
    ///
    /// <para>The exact question is <see cref="MetadataStatusVocabulary.Offers"/>
    /// rather than the looser <c>Recognises</c>, and why is recorded there: a
    /// browser matches a select's value against its options literally.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool StatusIsSelectable => Vocabulary.Offers(_status);

    /// <summary>The vocabulary's words, in the order it lists them. The value is
    /// authored, and the label is the same word — a status is already the reader's
    /// own term for the state, so there is nothing to translate.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> StatusOptions =>
        [.. Vocabulary.Values.Select(value => new SelectorOption(value, value))];

    private async Task SelectStatus(string status)
    {
        _status = status;

        if (OnStatusChanged.HasDelegate)
        {
            await OnStatusChanged.InvokeAsync(status);
        }
    }

    private string ClassValue => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? "knowledge-record" : $"knowledge-record {CssClass}";

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether there is a record here at all.
    /// <para>
    /// A block with nothing in it never drew one. What is new is a block that
    /// states only fields nobody asked to see: the group would be a border and an
    /// aria-label around an empty line. The heading counts as content in its own
    /// right, and has to — in a document the headline is where the chapter's
    /// heading is rendered, so a record that stood down for want of a status would
    /// take the heading off the page with it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool RendersAnything =>
        !Metadata.IsEmpty
        && (Heading is not null
            || !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_status)
            || (ShowFields && HasSecondaryFields));

    /// <summary>Whether anything but the status has a value. Status is drawn in
    /// the headline and nowhere else, so a block that states only a status has
    /// no list to draw — and an empty <c>&lt;dl&gt;</c> is a gap with a gap's
    /// spacing around it.</summary>
    private bool HasSecondaryFields =>
        Metadata.Related.Count > 0
        || Metadata.DependsOn.Count > 0
        || Metadata.Implements.Count > 0
        || Metadata.Aliases.Count > 0
        || Metadata.Alternatives.Count > 0
        || !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Kind)
        || !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Version)
        || !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Issue)
        || Metadata.Effort is not null
        || Metadata.Roadmap.Count > 0
        || Metadata.FeatureFlag.Count > 0
        || Metadata.Extra.Count > 0;
}
